{"name":"Oilseed Crops","hex":"#c2be0e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oilseed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The seed of any of several plants which are used commercially as a source of vegetable oil.","The plant that yields such seed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oilseed"},{"term":"crops","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of crop"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of crop"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crops"}],"entries":[{"title":"Vegetable oil","blocks":[{"text":"Vegetable oils, or vegetable fats, are oils extracted from seeds or from other parts of edible plants. Like animal fats, vegetable fats are mixtures of triglycerides. Soybean oil, canola oil, grapeseed oil, and cocoa butter are examples of seed oils, or fats from seeds. Olive oil, palm oil, and rice bran oil are examples of fats from other parts of plants. In common usage, vegetable oil may refer exclusively to vegetable fats which are liquid at room temperature. Vegetable oils are usually edible and are generally recognized as safe (GRAS) by the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA). There is no evidence that seed oils are unhealthy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Olive_oil_from_Oneglia.jpg/330px-Olive_oil_from_Oneglia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vegetable_oil","wikidata":"Q4739805"},{"title":"Crop","blocks":[{"text":"A crop is a plant or plant product harvested for human use. Crops are cultivated at scale to produce food, fiber, fuel, and other products. Crops have been central to human civilization since the first agricultural revolution, a key stage in the broader history of agriculture, when early societies domesticated plants for food and trade. Today, a small number of staple crops such as rice, wheat, maize, and sugarcane account for the majority of global production. Because of their economic importance, crops are studied within several scientific disciplines, including agronomy, agricultural science, horticulture, and forestry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Orange-tree-1117423.jpg/330px-Orange-tree-1117423.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crop","wikidata":"Q235352"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg/500px-Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg/1280px-Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg/500px-Peanut_oil_bottle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bottle of peanut oil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeanut_oil_bottle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg/500px-Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg/1280px-Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg/500px-Lavender_field_at_Hartley_Park_Farm_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1949000.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https: